<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chiang Saen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chiang Saen is an ancient town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> in the far north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, near the Golden Triangle where Thailand, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and Myanmar meet. Northern Thai chronicles make it the seat of Ngoenyang, the principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai</a>'s ancestors, before he moved south to found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-rai">Chiang Rai</a> and Chiang Mai. Rebuilt as a walled city in the 14th century, it was abandoned after Siam expelled its Burmese garrison in 1804.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chiang-saen.jpg" alt="Chiang Saen" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jpatokal · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ChiangSaen_Riverside.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
